 "ms_note": "Mass spectrometry is modelled as TWO independent platforms as of engine v2.9 (Seer Proteograph XT and Biognosys TrueDiscovery), each with its own accession-level coverage list from public/proteins.json. Both are independently PTM/proteoform-capable, so ms_fit=1 for any pair containing either.",
 "fairness_note": "Axis redundancy audit (15 pairs): coverage & class_balance correlate r=+0.93, essentially unchanged from the 10-pair engine. class_balance defaults to weight 0.5 so this redundancy is not double-counted (user-adjustable). No axis is privileged by default (all of coverage/complementarity/quality/cost start at weight 1; see default_philosophy). Under the representative priority sweep (924 scenarios spanning all four numeric coverage denominators), no pair reaches the 80% top-3 threshold required for Tier 1: Nomic Omni + Seer Proteograph XT leads on both win-rate (18.7%) and top-3 rate (48.5%), with SomaSeq + Seer Proteograph XT, Olink Explore HT + Seer Proteograph XT, and Nomic Omni + Biognosys TrueDiscovery clustering close behind as genuine Tier-2 contenders. The ranking is real about being weighting-dependent: which pair 'wins' changes substantially with the user's priorities and chosen coverage denominator (see ranking_sensitivity_note).",
 "quality_axes": {
  "intrinsic": {
   "dims": [
    "precision",
    "specificity",
    "sensitivity",
    "quantification_type",
    "pqtl_accuracy"
   ],
   "default_weight": 1.0,
   "label": "Intrinsic measurement properties",
   "note": "How good a single measurement is. THIS IS THE DEFAULT quality axis."
  },
  "maturity": {
   "dims": [
    "throughput",
    "evidence_depth",
    "sample_flexibility"
   ],
   "default_weight": 0.0,
   "label": "Platform maturity / operational",
   "note": "Adoption & practicality. OFF by default, it reflects how established a platform is, not its measurement properties."
  }
 },
 "quality_custom_weighting": "Users may override the grouping and set a custom importance weight (0 to 3) on any of the 8 quality sub-dimensions individually. Default = intrinsic dims at 1, maturity dims at 0.",
 "naming_note": "nELISA is Nomic's technology (= Nomic Omni). NULISA is Alamar's separate platform; different companies, do not conflate. Nomic (nELISA) has the top quantification_type score (4 = absolute pg/mL concentration); NULISA scores 2 (within-assay relative). Seer Proteograph XT and Biognosys TrueDiscovery are both independent mass-spectrometry platforms (see ms_note); they are no longer modelled as a single merged category as of engine v2.9.",
 "ranking_sensitivity_note": "Under default (equal) weights and the drug-target denominator: Nomic Omni + Seer Proteograph XT ranks highest on the composite (0.7178), followed by SomaSeq + Seer Proteograph XT (0.6424), Olink Explore HT + Seer Proteograph XT (0.6331), and Alamar NULISA + Seer Proteograph XT (0.6092); the gap between #1 and #4 is small relative to the sweep's spread. Across the full priority sweep, max win-share is 18.0% and max top-3 share is 47.5%, both held by Nomic Omni + Seer Proteograph XT; no pair reaches Tier 1 (top-3 >= 80%).",
 "default_philosophy": "All axes with a nonzero default weight (coverage, complementarity, quality, cost) start EQUAL at weight 1: no single axis is privileged by default. class_balance defaults to 0.5; sample_volume and ms_fit default to 0 and are opt-in only. Users set up to three priorities to raise the axes that matter to them; the tool does not presuppose that coverage (or any other axis) is what a given user cares about most.",
 "complementarity_note": "Complementarity is measured as NET-NEW targets added, not 1-Jaccard orthogonality, which rewards small panels for a high unique-fraction while contributing few targets in absolute terms. Net-new reflects how many targets the pairing actually lets you measure that a single platform can't. Example: Nomic Omni + Biognosys TrueDiscovery adds 140 net-new drug targets, the largest net-new figure of any Nomic Omni pairing, ahead of Nomic Omni + Seer Proteograph XT (91).",
